#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# prime_nu

#% text
# `prime_nu(n)` gives the number of distinct primes dividing `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `prime_nu(1)` is 0. The count needs the factorisation of `n`.

#% item
# `prime_nu` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
prime_nu(12)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
prime_nu(30)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
prime_nu(64)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
prime_nu(range(10))
#% output
#= [0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 1, 1, 1, 2]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Compared with `prime_omega`, which counts multiplicity:

#% cell
[prime_nu(720), prime_omega(720)]
#% output
#= [3, 7]

#% text
# The number of square-free divisors is `2^prime_nu(n)`:

#% cell
[count_if(divisors(360), is_square_free), 2^prime_nu(360)]
#% output
#= [8, 8]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `prime_omega`, `factor_integer`, `moebius_mu`
